# Gantry — Camera, pointer, and key actions

This file defines how the player operates the game: the registered key
actions, the orbit camera, pointer picking on the build screen, and what each
screen does with them. What the edits themselves mean is `specs/structure.md`
and `specs/program.md`; what each screen shows is `specs/ui.md`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The runtime layer you write delivers the input: it maps the key bindings below
to the actions the game reads, and it reports the pointer's position in logical
stage units along with its press and release edges.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine delivers the input: the game registers the actions
below against their bindings, and the engine reports the pointer's position in
logical stage units along with its press and release edges. The engine's own
documentation defines the input API; this file states what Gantry registers
and does with it.
{{/if}}

## The actions

| Action | Binding (`KeyboardEvent.code`) | Does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `up`, `down`, `left`, `right` | `ArrowUp`, `ArrowDown`, `ArrowLeft`, `ArrowRight` | menu navigation; camera orbit on the 3D screens |
| `zoom-in`, `zoom-out` | `Equal`, `Minus` | camera distance |
| `confirm` | `Enter` | take the highlighted menu item |
| `back` | `Escape` | leave the screen, as `specs/ui.md` states per screen; abort on the run screen; cancel a pending first node on the build screen |
| `tool-strut` | `Digit1` | select the strut tool |
| `tool-cable` | `Digit2` | select the cable tool |
| `tool-rail` | `Digit3` | select the rail tool |
| `tool-ring` | `Digit4` | select the ring tool |
| `tool-counterweight` | `Digit5` | select the counterweight tool |
| `tool-delete` | `Digit6` | select the delete tool |
| `undo` | `KeyZ` | undo the most recent structure edit |
| `check` | `KeyC` | the static check, on the build screen (`specs/instrumentation.md`) |
| `program` | `KeyP` | build screen to program screen |
| `build` | `KeyB` | program screen to build screen |
| `run` | `KeyG` | start the run, from build or program (`specs/program.md`) |
| `speed` | `KeyS` | cycle `RUN_SPEEDS` on the run screen |
| `mute` | `KeyM` | toggle sound, on every screen |

The tool, undo, check, and screen-switch actions apply where the table says
and do nothing elsewhere; the menus are driven by the four directions,
`confirm`, and `back` alone.

## The camera

The build, program, and run screens show the 3D yard through an orbit camera:
it looks at `CAMERA_TARGET` (`(0, 6, 0)`) from a yaw, a pitch, and a distance.

| Figure | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Start pose | yaw `CAMERA_START_YAW` (`45`), pitch `CAMERA_START_PITCH` (`30`), distance `CAMERA_START_DIST` (`40`) |
| Pitch limits | `CAMERA_PITCH_MIN` (`10`) to `CAMERA_PITCH_MAX` (`80`) degrees |
| Distance limits | `CAMERA_DIST_MIN` (`10`) to `CAMERA_DIST_MAX` (`80`) |
| Key orbit rate | `ORBIT_KEY_RATE` (`90`) deg/s |
| Zoom rate | `ZOOM_RATE` (`20`) units/s |
| Drag orbit | `ORBIT_PER_PX` (`0.25`) degrees per logical pixel |

The four direction actions orbit, `left` and `right` in yaw and `up` and
`down` in pitch, and the zoom actions move the distance, each against the
frame's delta time. Dragging the pointer orbits too: a press that is not a
click (below) turns yaw and pitch by `ORBIT_PER_PX` per logical pixel of
pointer movement. The camera pose persists across the three screens and
resets to the start pose when a site is opened.

## Clicks and picking

On the build screen the pointer both orbits and edits, told apart by
movement: a press that moves less than `CLICK_SLOP` (`6`) logical pixels
before its release is a click, applied at the release position; anything
longer is an orbit drag and edits nothing.

A click picks by screen distance:

- A node pick considers every lattice node in the envelope, projected to the
  stage; the candidate is the nearest within `NODE_PICK_PX` (`20`) logical
  pixels of the click, ties going to the node nearest the camera. The
  candidate under the pointer is highlighted before the click, so the player
  sees what a click would take.
- A member pick considers every member's projected segment; the candidate is
  the nearest within `MEMBER_PICK_PX` (`12`) logical pixels.

A click with no candidate in range does nothing.

## The build tools

The selected tool decides what a click does. Every edit passes through the
rules in `specs/structure.md`, and a refused edit changes nothing on screen
beyond the refusal being visible in the moment (`specs/ui.md`).

- Strut, cable, rail: the first click picks a node and holds it pending,
  visibly marked; the second click on another node places the member between
  them and clears the pending node. A second click on the pending node itself,
  or `back`, clears it without placing. A second click that the rules refuse
  leaves the pending node held, so a slip does not cost the selection.
  Switching tools keeps a pending node; it belongs to the placement, not the
  material.
- Ring: a click places the ring by its base corner at the picked node.
- Counterweight: a click on a node without a counterweight places one there; a
  click on a node carrying one removes it.
- Delete: a click removes what it picks, the nearest in screen distance of a
  member within `MEMBER_PICK_PX`, a counterweight within `NODE_PICK_PX` of its
  node, or the ring within `NODE_PICK_PX` of any of its eight flange nodes.

`undo` reverses the most recent structure-changing edit, as far back as the
site was opened, exactly as `specs/structure.md` states.

## Editing the tape

The program screen edits the tape with the pointer and the menu actions. The
exact widgets are the build's design; what the player can do is fixed: add a
step, a move or an action; give a move its commands, each an axis, a target,
and a rate the editor accepts under `specs/program.md`; edit, reorder, and
remove existing steps; and read the whole tape in order. Every edit is
reflected in the state's program (`specs/state.md`).

## The run screen

The run screen takes `speed`, `mute`, and `back` alone: watching, not
steering, with `back` aborting as `specs/program.md` states.
